WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.377
Authentication establishes who is making a
request,

2
00:00:03.457 --> 00:00:07.102
or at least which authenticated principal
the server has accepted.

3
00:00:07.182 --> 00:00:10.442
Object-level authorization answers a
different question:

4
00:00:10.522 --> 00:00:14.103
may that principal perform this action on
this particular object?

5
00:00:14.183 --> 00:00:18.786
A valid session cookie or correctly signed
JSON Web Token supplies identity context.

6
00:00:18.866 --> 00:00:23.086
It does not grant blanket access to every
invoice, project, ticket, document,

7
00:00:23.166 --> 00:00:26.299
user profile, or tenant record reachable
through the API.

8
00:00:26.379 --> 00:00:30.407
Object references appear in more places
than a route such as slash API slash

9
00:00:30.487 --> 00:00:32.533
invoices slash three eight one seven.

10
00:00:32.613 --> 00:00:36.002
They can arrive in query parameters,
request bodies, headers,

11
00:00:36.082 --> 00:00:39.854
GraphQL-style arguments, filenames, slugs,
nested routes, arrays,

12
00:00:39.934 --> 00:00:41.213
and workflow commands.

13
00:00:41.293 --> 00:00:45.577
If changing one valid reference to another
causes the server to read or modify an

14
00:00:45.657 --> 00:00:48.342
object outside the requester's permitted
scope,

15
00:00:48.422 --> 00:00:51.747
the application has an object-level
authorization failure.

16
00:00:51.827 --> 00:00:55.215
Access across peer users is horizontal
privilege escalation.

17
00:00:55.295 --> 00:00:58.748
Access across organizations is commonly
cross-tenant access.

18
00:00:58.828 --> 00:01:03.368
An object operation can also become
vertical escalation when a general user reaches

19
00:01:03.448 --> 00:01:08.115
an approval, administrative, or
state-changing action reserved for a stronger role.

20
00:01:08.195 --> 00:01:12.415
Random identifiers reduce casual discovery
but do not establish permission.

21
00:01:12.495 --> 00:01:16.395
A UUID can leak through logs, browser
history, shared links, analytics,

22
00:01:16.475 --> 00:01:18.649
support messages, or another endpoint.

23
00:01:18.729 --> 00:01:21.286
Once obtained, it is still an object
reference.

24
00:01:21.366 --> 00:01:25.778
Treat unpredictability as defense-in-depth
and require the same authorization

25
00:01:25.858 --> 00:01:28.927
decision for integers, UUIDs, slugs, and
opaque tokens.

26
00:01:29.007 --> 00:01:32.332
The check must follow the object through
its full API surface.

27
00:01:32.412 --> 00:01:36.248
Reading, updating, deleting, exporting,
downloading an attachment,

28
00:01:36.328 --> 00:01:39.397
adding a comment, approving, transferring,
archiving,

29
00:01:39.477 --> 00:01:43.313
and resending can expose different
capabilities over the same record.

30
00:01:43.393 --> 00:01:48.125
Object-level authorization is therefore an
application design property expressed in

31
00:01:48.205 --> 00:01:50.698
routes, services, policies, and data
access.

32
00:01:50.778 --> 00:01:54.998
It is not a single Express switch that can
be enabled without understanding the

33
00:01:55.078 --> 00:01:57.060
target object and requested action.
